---
// Methods: definitions, baseline, channels, each dataset's method, assumptions, uncertainties,
// flags, frozen tests, display choices and known gaps. Facts here are read from the packages'
// SOURCE.md files and the decision log; dataset-specific numbers are pulled from the registry.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import L from '../components/L.astro';
import Layer from '../components/Layer.astro';
import { loadAtlas } from '../lib/atlas.mjs';
import { datasetLabel, assumptionLabel, fmtParam, rangeLabel, datasetCount, countWord, joinAnd, plottedSystemCount } from '../lib/labels.mjs';
const atlas = loadAtlas();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const REPO = 'https://github.com/extragravitation/atlas';
const D = 'https://github.com/extragravitation/atlas/blob/main/docs/decisions.md';
const adj = atlas.datasets.flatMap((d) => d.assumptions.filter((a) => a.adjustable && a.status === 'confirmed').map((a) => ({ a, d, e: a.datasets[d.key], k: assumptionLabel(a.id).kind })));
const nAll = atlas.datasets.reduce((n, d) => n + d.measurements.rows.length, 0), nSparc = atlas.datasets.filter((d) => d.name === 'aeg-d-sparc').reduce((n, d) => n + d.measurements.rows.length, 0);
const nDs = datasetCount(atlas.datasets);
// Lensing datasets, counted and described from the packages: stacked (no systems) or individual (projected at R_E).
const lensDs = atlas.datasets.filter((d) => d.measurements.rows.some((r) => r.method_class === 'lensing'));
const lensText = lensDs.length === 0 ? 'No lensing dataset yet.' : `${countWord(lensDs.length).replace(/^./, (c) => c.toUpperCase())} lensing dataset${lensDs.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}: ${joinAnd(lensDs.map((d) => `${datasetLabel(d.name).t} (${d.systems ? 'individual systems' + (d.measurements.rows.some((r) => r.r_kind === 'projected') ? ', projected values at the Einstein radius' : '') : 'stacked averages'})`))}.`;
const sysCounts = (d) => `${d.systems.rows.length} recorded, ${plottedSystemCount(d)} plotted`;
// Plain list of the lensing datasets, from the same packages.
const lensTextP = lensDs.length === 0 ? 'No light-bending data yet.' : `Light-bending data so far: ${joinAnd(lensDs.map((d) => d.systems ? 'single galaxies that bend light into rings and arcs' : 'averages over many lone galaxies'))}.`;
const toc = [['stance', 'Stance'], ['quantities', 'What is measured'], ['baseline', 'The baseline'], ['channels', 'Two channels'], ['datasets', 'Each dataset'], ['conversion', 'Conversion steps'], ['assumptions', 'Assumptions'], ['uncertainties', 'Uncertainties'], ['flags', 'Flags and known issues'], ['tests', 'Frozen tests'], ['lenses', 'Lenses'], ['display', 'Display choices'], ['speed', 'Orbital-speed view'], ['gaps', 'Limitations and gaps'], ['vocabulary', 'Vocabulary']];
---
<Base title="Methods" active="methods">
  <div class="doc">
    <main class="prose">
      <p class="kicker"><L t="Methods" p="How it works" /></p>
      <h1><L t="Definitions, baseline and methods" p="How the Atlas works" /></h1>
      <Layer as="p" class="lede"><Fragment slot="t">The Atlas compares two accelerations at the same place in the same system: the one observed, and the one Newtonian gravity gives from the matter that has been detected. It describes where and by how much they differ. It does not say why.</Fragment><Fragment slot="p">At each place in a galaxy or cluster, the Atlas compares the pull we observe with the pull that the matter we can detect should give. It shows where and by how much they differ. It does not say why.</Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="stance"><L t="Stance: cause-agnostic and reference-explicit" p="Where we stand" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>No comparison of this kind is free of theory. The Atlas compares observed quantities with a <b>declared reference calculation</b>: Newtonian gravity on the baryons each source detected, and General Relativity for turning a lensing signal into an acceleration. Every step from observation to comparison is named, including those that cannot be adjusted, and the observations themselves are kept, so any account can be checked against the data rather than against the Atlas’s conversion (D-029, D-030). The Atlas takes no side on what causes a difference from the reference. Neutrality is sought through rigour in the record and symmetry in presentation: several lenses on the same data, the same template for every account, and where the discrepancy is absent shown as prominently as where it is present.</p>
        <p>The site has five layers with their own rules: the <b>record</b> (data packages; nothing interpretive), <b>lenses</b> (views of the same data), <b>patterns</b> (descriptive regularities and where they fail), <b>accounts and tests</b> (explanations from their proponents’ published claims; predictions frozen before the data), and <b>exploration</b> (labelled, never cited as a finding).</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Any comparison needs a yardstick. Ours is stated openly: Newton’s gravity from the matter that was detected. We say exactly how each number was worked out and keep the original measurements, so anyone can check them. We do not say what causes the extra pull.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="quantities"><L t="What is measured" p="The two numbers" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <ul>
          <li><b>g_obs</b>, the observed acceleration: inferred from motions (dynamics) or from light-bending (lensing), as the source measured it.</li>
          <li><b>g_bar</b>, the baryonic acceleration: Newtonian gravity from the baryonic matter the source detected (stars and gas), at the same radius.</li>
          <li><b>g_obs / g_bar</b>, the acceleration discrepancy. Equal to 1 where Newtonian gravity on detected matter accounts for the observed acceleration.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>g_obs and g_bar are themselves inferred from what was observed: rotation speeds, velocity dispersions, X-ray temperatures and densities, lensing signals, starlight and gas. System pages show these source quantities in an <b>As measured</b> panel; under data standard 2.0 (D-029) every package records them with the processing steps that led to them.</p>
        <p>All accelerations are in m s⁻², radii in kpc. The conventional name for the same comparison is the "mass discrepancy"; the Atlas does not use it, because it presumes the difference is due to mass.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <ul>
          <li><b>Observed pull</b>: how strong gravity is, worked out from how things move or how light bends.</li>
          <li><b>Pull from detected matter</b>: how strong gravity should be from the stars and gas that were detected, using Newton’s law.</li>
          <li><b>Extra pull</b>: how many times stronger the observed pull is. A value of 1 means no extra pull.</li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="baseline"><L t="The baseline: Newtonian gravity on detected matter" p="What we compare against" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Every comparison is against Newtonian gravity acting on the baryons each source detected, computed by the source or from the source’s published components. Components are kept separate in the tables (stars, disc and bulge where given, cold gas, hot gas) and summed into g_bar. What counts as detected is stated per dataset below; matter a source did not measure is not added. On the map, the dashed line g_obs = g_bar marks this baseline.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>We always compare with Newton’s gravity from the stars and gas that each study actually measured. We do not add anything they did not measure. On the map, the dashed line shows where Newton’s gravity from the detected matter matches all of the pull.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="channels"><L t="Two channels: dynamics and lensing" p="Two ways to measure the pull" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Dynamics (rotation curves, hot gas in hydrostatic balance) and gravitational lensing measure g_obs in physically different ways, with different assumptions. They are kept as separate channels in the data and are never combined by default. Marker shape distinguishes them on the map.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>The pull can be measured from how stars, gas and galaxies move, or from how much the gravity bends light from things behind. These are different methods with different assumptions, so we keep them apart.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="datasets"><L t="Each dataset" p="Where the numbers come from" /></h2>
      <p class="small"><L t="As read at source and recorded in each package’s SOURCE.md; full details on the dataset pages." p="Full details are on each dataset’s page." /></p>
      {atlas.datasets.map((d) => { const lab = datasetLabel(d.name); return <>
        <h3><a href={`${base}datasets/${d.name}/${d.version}/`}>{lab.t}</a> · <L t={lab.method_t} p={lab.method_p} /></h3>
        {d.name === 'aeg-d-sparc' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>{d.systems.rows.length} disc galaxies (Lelli, McGaugh &amp; Schombert 2016). g_obs = V_obs² / R from the rotation curve. g_bar = (|V_gas|V_gas + Υ_disk |V_disk|V_disk + Υ_bul |V_bul|V_bul) / R from the 3.6 μm photometry and the atomic-gas maps (gas mass includes a factor 1.33 for helium). g_bar does not depend on the adopted distance; g_obs does.</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>{d.systems.rows.length} spinning disc galaxies. The observed pull comes from how fast stars and gas go round; the pull from detected matter comes from the starlight and the hydrogen gas.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.key === 'aeg-d-xcop/v2' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>{d.systems.rows.length} massive galaxy clusters (X-COP), of which the five with stellar data have values (Eckert et al. 2022). g_obs and g_bar are taken as the source released them, at the radii of its data: g_obs from the non-parametric hydrostatic reconstruction, g_bar = G (M_gas + M★,BCG + M★,sat)(&lt;r) / r² (D-089, D-090). The other seven clusters are recorded, not plotted: the source gives no stellar mass for them.</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>Big clusters of galaxies. The observed pull comes from how the hot gas between the galaxies is held in place; the pull from detected matter comes from that hot gas plus the stars. Both are as the scientists released them.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.key === 'aeg-d-xcop/v1' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>{d.systems.rows.length} massive galaxy clusters (X-COP). g_obs = G M_HSE(&lt;r) / r², from the X-ray hydrostatic mass profile (forward method, Ettori et al. 2019). g_bar = G (M_gas + M_★)(&lt;r) / r², from the hot-gas mass (Eckert et al. 2019) and stellar mass (Ghizzardi et al. 2020) profiles. For 5 clusters without measured stellar profiles, the stellar mass is modelled and kept only where the model reproduces the measured clusters (D-023).</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>{d.systems.rows.length} big clusters of galaxies. The observed pull comes from how the hot gas between the galaxies is held in place; the pull from detected matter comes from that hot gas plus the stars.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.name === 'aeg-d-gc-baumgardt' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>Milky Way globular clusters ({sysCounts(d)}), one value each at the 3D half-light radius r½ (Baumgardt et al. database; Harris 2010; McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel 2005). g_obs = 3 ⟨σ²⟩ / r½ from the light-weighted line-of-sight velocity dispersion; g_bar = G (½ L_V Υ_V) / r½² from the integrated V luminosity and a population mass-to-light ratio. Gas is left out of g_bar on a published limit for the kind (D-036 #4). The steps are under <a href="#conversion">Conversion steps</a>.</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>Old, dense balls of stars in our galaxy ({sysCounts(d)}). The observed pull comes from how fast the stars swarm; the pull from detected matter comes from the starlight. Gas is left out, because published limits for these clusters are small.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.name === 'aeg-d-dwarfs' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>Dwarf galaxies of the Local Group ({sysCounts(d)}), one value each at the 3D half-light radius r½, read from the original papers that the Local Volume Database (Pace 2025) indexes. g_obs = 3 σ² / r½; g_bar = G (½ L_V Υ_V + M_gas(&lt;r½)) / r½², with atomic gas from Putman et al. 2021 where it is measured. The steps are under <a href="#conversion">Conversion steps</a>.</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>Small, faint galaxies near our own ({sysCounts(d)}). The observed pull comes from how fast the stars swarm; the pull from detected matter comes from the starlight and, where it was measured, the hydrogen gas.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.name === 'aeg-d-strong-lensing' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>Galaxy-scale strong lenses whose lens is an early-type galaxy ({sysCounts(d)}), one projected value each at the Einstein radius R_E. g_obs = G M_E / R_E² from the lensing mass inside R_E; g_bar = G (M★ + M_gas)(&lt;R_E) / R_E², with population-synthesis stellar masses projected into the same cylinder and the registered gas term (D-064, D-076). The steps are under <a href="#conversion">Conversion steps</a>.</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>Single galaxies whose pull bends the light of a galaxy behind them into a ring or arcs ({sysCounts(d)}). Both pulls are measured inside the ring, seen in projection: the observed pull from the bending, the pull from detected matter from the stars and a small allowance for gas.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
        {d.name === 'aeg-d-kids1000-rar' && <Layer><Fragment slot="t"><p>{d.measurements.rows.length} stacked bins of isolated galaxies (Brouwer et al. 2021). g_obs = 4 G ΔΣ from the excess surface density (singular-isothermal-sphere deprojection, validated by the authors against a shape-free method). g_bar from stellar mass plus cold gas treated as a point mass. These are averages over many lens galaxies, not single systems. The authors’ ±0.2 dex stellar-mass systematic is stated in words, not drawn (D-021).</p></Fragment><Fragment slot="p"><p>Averages over many lone galaxies. The pull is measured from how much they bend light from galaxies far behind them. Each point is an average, not one galaxy.</p></Fragment></Layer>}
      </>; })}

      <h2 id="conversion"><L t="Conversion steps and constants" p="How the numbers are worked out" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p class="small">This section was added after the independent audit found steps that the written methods did not state (audit findings M1–M7; <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/audit/AUDIT_RESULT.md`}>audit result</a>, D-107). It describes what the conversions do. It does not change any value. Each package's own method file is completed when the package is re-issued.</p>
        <h3>Constants and units (all datasets)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>G</b> = 6.67430 × 10⁻¹¹ m³ kg⁻¹ s⁻² (CODATA 2018; the same value as CODATA 2022).</li>
          <li><b>M☉</b> = 1.98840987 × 10³⁰ kg (IAU 2015 nominal).</li>
          <li><b>1 pc</b> = 3.0856775814913673 × 10¹⁶ m (IAU 2015 B2); 1 kpc = 10³ pc; 1 Mpc = 10⁶ pc.</li>
          <li><b>c</b> = 299 792 458 m s⁻¹ (exact).</li>
          <li><b>Solar absolute V magnitude</b> = +4.85, as adopted by McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel (2005).</li>
          <li><b>Where the constants come from in the code.</b> SPARC, X-COP, KiDS-1000 and the globular clusters take them from astropy (version 6.1 or later). The dwarfs and lenses state them in code. All give the values above.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>KiDS-1000</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>g_obs = 4 G ΔΣ / bias, where ΔΣ is the released <code>ESD_t</code> (h70 M☉ pc⁻², h = 0.7) and bias is the released <code>bias(1+K)</code> column, as the release README instructs.</li>
          <li>g_bar is the released first column. It is the arithmetic midpoint of the source's §2.1 bin, the g_bar at which the lenses were binned (stars plus cold gas, from each lens's stellar mass).</li>
          <li>The hot-gas variant file adds the authors' assumed hot-gas component to g_bar. That file is recorded and labelled as a variant.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Globular clusters</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Profile.</b> The line-of-sight dispersion profile is taken from the Baumgardt et al. database (radial-velocity bins first, D-042).</li>
          <li><b>Bin edges.</b> The first edge is 0. Each inner edge is the midpoint between consecutive mean radii. The last edge is r_n + (r_n − r_n−1)/2.</li>
          <li><b>Weights.</b> Each bin's weight w_i is the fraction of the cluster's total light projected inside its annulus, under the King (1966) fit of McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel (2005).</li>
          <li><b>Dispersion.</b> ⟨σ²⟩ = Σ w_i σ_i² / Σ w_i. Coverage is Σ w_i, and a point is plotted only if coverage ≥ 0.80.</li>
          <li><b>Accelerations.</b> g_obs = 3 ⟨σ²⟩ / r½ and g_bar = G (½ L_V Υ_V) / r½², with r½ = 4/3 R_h (Wolf et al. 2010).
            <ul>
              <li>R_h is the McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel value, rescaled to the Harris (2010) distance.</li>
              <li>L_V comes from the Harris integrated M_V.</li>
              <li>Υ_V is the McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel (M/L)₁.</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Dwarf galaxies</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Accelerations.</b> The same estimator as for globular clusters: g_obs = 3 σ² / r½ and g_bar = G (½ L_V Υ_V + M_gas(&lt;r½)) / r½², with r½ = 4/3 R_h.</li>
          <li><b>Radius.</b> A semi-major r_h is circularised as R_h = a √(1 − ε). A radius the source prints as already circularised (for example an "effective geometric radius") is used as printed (D-052).</li>
          <li><b>Mass-to-light ratio.</b> Υ_V is interpolated in [Fe/H] in the McLaughlin &amp; van der Marel (2005) table. The [Fe/H] is the mean spectroscopic value from the original paper that the Local Volume Database cites (D-057). If there is none, the photometric or isochrone original is used. If that is missing too, the dispersion paper's value is used (D-059).</li>
          <li><b>Choosing among values.</b> Where a source prints several values, the one the source adopts is used. Where the same value is printed at two precisions, the more precise printing is used; for example, Tucana's σ = 14.35 km/s comes from the Fig. 5 panel of Gregory et al. 2019.</li>
          <li><b>Gas.</b> Gas marked "absent (measured limit)" enters g_bar as zero, and the limit is shown in the point's notes.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Strong lenses</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Distances.</b> Flat ΛCDM in each source's own cosmology, with radiation neglected: D_A from the comoving distance, and D_ls = (D_C(z_s) − D_C(z_l)) / (1 + z_s).</li>
          <li><b>Einstein mass.</b> M_E = θ_E² c² D_s D_l / (4 G D_ls), with R_E = θ_E D_l. Where the source prints M_E, the printed value is used.</li>
          <li><b>Stellar mass.</b> M★(&lt;R_E) is the total stellar mass times the projected light fraction inside R_E, for the source's light profile (de Vaucouleurs, n = 4).</li>
          <li><b>Gas.</b> Cold gas inside R_E is the registered term of 0.12 % of M★(&lt;R_E), with a range of 0.015–2.5 % (D-076).</li>
          <li><b>Accelerations.</b> g = G M / R_E² on both sides.</li>
          <li><b>ESO 325-G004 (SNL-0).</b> The CMB-frame redshift (0.0347) is used. The registered gas term applies there too.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>X-COP</h3>
        <p>The current version follows the source's released accelerations (above). The superseded v1 conversion is described in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/packages/aeg-d-xcop/v1/SOURCE.md`}>its SOURCE.md</a>. Its row locators count rows from 1.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Every number uses the same physical constants: the standard values of the strength of gravity, the mass of the Sun, the length of a parsec and the speed of light. For each kind of system, this section spells out every step from the published numbers to the two pulls, so that anyone can redo them. It was added after an outside-style audit showed that some steps had not been written down.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="assumptions"><L t="Assumptions and sliders" p="Assumptions you can change" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Each value rests on assumptions. Those with a published default and a published range are adjustable; the effect of a change is computed from the data by the formulas in the assumption registry (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/assumptions.json`}>data/assumptions.json</a>), executed identically by the pipeline and in the browser (a build check confirms they agree on every measurement). Nothing is approximated for display.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Whole-dataset</b> assumptions take one value for every system in a dataset, as the sources adopt them. Changing one on any page changes it everywhere.</li>
          <li><b>Per-system</b> assumptions (distance and inclination) move one system within its own published one-sigma error, on that system’s page.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A bar at the top of every page lists any assumption that differs from its default, with a one-click reset. Downloads always hold the source’s defaults.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Some numbers depend on choices, such as how much mass a given amount of starlight stands for. Where the published studies give a range, you can move a slider within it and see the effect, computed from the data. Some sliders change a whole dataset; distance and tilt change one galaxy at a time, on its own page.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>
      <div tabindex="0" class="tablebox" style="max-height:none"><table>
        <thead><tr><th class="l">Assumption</th><th class="l">Dataset</th><th class="l">Scope</th><th>Default</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{adj.map(({ a, d, e, k }) => <tr><td class="l"><L t={assumptionLabel(a.id).t} p={assumptionLabel(a.id).p} /></td><td class="l">{datasetLabel(d.name).t}</td><td class="l">{a.scope === 'system' ? 'per system' : 'whole dataset'}</td><td>{fmtParam(k, e.default)}</td><td>{rangeLabel(a.id, e)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="small">Sources for every default and range are on the dataset pages and in the registry. Decisions: <a href={`${D}#d-014--v01-assumption-sliders`}>D-014</a>, <a href={`${D}#d-022--x-cop-assumptions-defaults-and-ranges`}>D-022</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="uncertainties"><L t="Uncertainties" p="How sure the numbers are" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <ul>
          <li><b>SPARC.</b> g_obs: δg_obs = g_obs √[(2δV/V)² + (2δi/tan i)² + (δD/D)²] (arXiv:1610.08981, eq. 2). g_bar: 10 % on the gas and 25 % on the stellar term, combined in quadrature (Lelli et al. 2017, arXiv:1610.08981, §2.1.2), as recorded in the package.</li>
          <li><b>X-COP.</b> g_obs: the source’s one-sigma mass errors. g_bar: the source’s gas errors and stellar uncertainty bounds, combined in quadrature separately below and above; modelled stellar masses carry no error.</li>
          <li><b>KiDS-1000.</b> g_obs: from the source’s one-sigma excess-surface-density error (bias-corrected, as released); the full released covariance is in the download. g_bar: no uncertainty released; the ±0.2 dex systematic is stated on every point.</li>
          <li><b>Globular clusters, dwarf galaxies and strong lenses.</b> Each uncertainty is listed with its scale, size and source in the dataset page's <i>Uncertainties by scale</i> table, from the package's uncertainty-terms table (data standard 2.2.0).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Error bars on the figures carry the terms that apply to one point only. Terms shared by a whole system or dataset move many points together, so they are listed on the dataset pages rather than drawn (D-083). Where a source gives no uncertainty, none is drawn, and the page says so. Error bars are one sigma. The system pages also draw the full published range of each assumption, independent of the current settings.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Most numbers come with an uncertainty, shown as a bar or a shaded band. Some do not: where a study gives none, none is drawn, and we say so. Uncertainties that move a whole galaxy or a whole dataset at once are listed on the dataset pages instead of drawn as bars. On each system’s page you can also see how far the values could move across the full published range of each assumption.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="flags"><L t="Flags and known issues" p="Known problems" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Measurements with a reliability question are flagged in the data and ringed on every figure: <code>reproduction_mismatch</code> (the measurement does not reproduce the source’s own published reference) and <code>source_quality_low</code> (the source’s own lowest quality class). Other flags are informational and listed in the tables. Flagged measurements are never removed. Each issue is listed on the <a href={`${base}known-issues/`}>Known issues</a> page (D-020).</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Points with a known problem stay on the charts, with a ring around them, and each problem is explained on the <a href={`${base}known-issues/`}>Known issues</a> page.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="tests"><L t="Frozen tests" p="Tests we locked in advance" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Before a dataset is converted, a reproduction test is written that compares the conversion with the source’s own published values, and its file hash is recorded. The test is then run once and its result published, pass or fail (D-005, D-019). A failed test is never re-scored; a package that fails it ships only if its conversion integrity is exact, with the mismatched measurements flagged. The same applies to any test or prediction the Atlas makes, and to predictions deposited by others in the <a href={`${base}tests/`}>test ledger</a>. The choice of datasets follows a selection protocol fixed before new data are converted (D-028). Exploratory work is labelled exploratory.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>We write each test down and lock it before running it, so the result cannot be bent to fit. Results are published whether they pass or fail. Anyone can lock in a prediction for data not yet added: see <a href={`${base}tests/`}>Tests</a>.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="lenses"><L t="Lenses" p="Different views of the same data" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>Each lens is a different framing of the same measurements (D-030); none is privileged as the true picture, and each comes from a research tradition.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Acceleration</b>: g_obs against g_bar. This plane is the one used in published studies of the radial acceleration relation.</li>
          <li><b>Mass</b>: spherical-equivalent enclosed mass M(&lt;r) = g r² / G, observed against baryonic. Enclosed-mass profiles and dynamical-to-baryonic mass ratios are standard in mass modelling. For flattened systems the spherical-equivalent mass is a convention, not the true enclosed mass; the ratio at each point equals g_obs / g_bar.</li>
          <li><b>Scale</b>: log (g_obs / g_bar) against radius, across all scales.</li>
          <li><b>Speed</b>: equivalent circular speed √(g_obs r) against radius; for disc galaxies, their rotation curves.</li>
          <li><b>Surface density</b>: needs the observables recorded under data standard 2.0 and arrives with the next packages.</li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Different research traditions look at the same measurements in different ways: as pulls, as masses, against distance, or as orbital speeds. The Atlas shows them all, side by side, and says where each view comes from.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="display"><L t="Display choices" p="Why the charts look the way they do" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <ul>
          <li>Several lenses show the same measurements (below). The map opens on g_obs against g_bar with the line g_obs = g_bar (D-012): it shows the two measured quantities without transforming either, and the reference line is the Atlas’s own baseline.</li>
          <li>No fitted relation is drawn (D-026): the data and the Newtonian baseline are shown, nothing else.</li>
          <li>Points by default; tracks join a system’s points in order of radius on request. The legend states how many points come from how many systems, so densely sampled systems do not read as a population (D-027).</li>
          <li>Continuous properties are shown with continuous colour scales; no class label carries the result.</li>
          <li><b>Systematics checks</b> (D-033): the map can colour each measurement by a property its source gives (inclination, distance method, the source's quality flag, distance, redshift). If the comparison were affected by that property, the colours would separate at fixed g_bar. No fit is made and no pass or fail is claimed.</li>
          <li>All current data are in the nearby universe; cosmic epoch is a documented gap for v1.0 (D-033; see Coverage).</li>
          <li>Measurements with g_bar ≤ 0 (a component pulling outward at that radius, as the source gives it) cannot be placed on logarithmic axes; they are listed under the map and shown on linear axes on their system pages.</li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>The map opens on the simplest picture: observed pull against the pull from detected matter. We do not draw any fitted curve, so you see the data and the Newton line only.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="speed"><L t="Orbital-speed view" p="Pull as a speed" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <p>System pages can show any acceleration g at radius r as the equivalent circular speed V = √(g r). For rotating discs this is the familiar rotation curve. For clusters it is the speed a circular orbit would need, not a measured speed. Speeds from separate components add in quadrature, so components are drawn as separate lines in this view.</p>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <p>Any pull can be turned into the speed something would need to orbit at that distance. For spinning galaxies that is the speed actually measured; for clusters it is only a way of picturing the pull.</p>
      </Fragment></Layer>

      <h2 id="gaps"><L t="Limitations and gaps" p="What is missing" /></h2>
      <Layer><Fragment slot="t">
        <ul>
          <li>{nDs.replace(/^./, (c) => c.toUpperCase())} so far. The first public release (v1.0) covers every kind of system in the taxonomy, each packaged or shown as a documented gap, including the high-acceleration end (the Solar System as a limit; globular clusters) and the lowest-acceleration individual systems (D-028; see <a href={`${base}coverage/`}>Coverage</a>).</li>
          <li>Disc galaxies dominate the current measurements ({nSparc.toLocaleString('en')} of {nAll.toLocaleString('en')}).</li>
          <li>{lensText}</li>
          <li>KiDS-bright: the authors state their isolation criterion becomes unreliable for g_bar ≲ 10⁻¹³ m s⁻².</li>
          <li>X-COP: hydrostatic masses assume the hot gas is in balance; the bias slider spans the published range.</li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment><Fragment slot="p">
        <ul>
          <li>{nDs.replace(/^./, (c) => c.toUpperCase())} so far. The full first release will cover every kind of object, each either measured or listed as a gap. Groups of galaxies, for example, are not in yet.</li>
          <li>Most measurements so far come from spinning disc galaxies ({nSparc.toLocaleString('en')} of {nAll.toLocaleString('en')}).</li>
          <li>{lensTextP}</li>
          <li>For the brighter KiDS-1000 sample, the survey team say their way of picking lone galaxies stops being reliable where the pull from detected matter is weakest.</li>
          <li>For X-COP, the masses assume the hot gas is held in balance; a slider covers the published range of how far off that may be.</li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment></Layer>

